  "body": "The macOS packaging step can sign the app and its three executables with Developer ID. It can submit the app and the DMG to the Apple notary service. It staples the app before it makes the final ZIP. It staples and validates the DMG before publishing either archive.\n\nWith no signing inputs, the packager keeps the ad hoc path and reports it. An incomplete set of inputs stops packaging. The release workflow passes the five optional secrets only to the packaging step. A private temporary keychain holds the certificate. The script removes temporary credentials on exit. The release triggers and permissions stay the same.\n\n## Verification\n\nOn commit `e536469d0`, all five packaging fixture tests passed, including six simulated failure cases. `test_signed_zip_and_dmg_include_validated_tickets` checks the two archives. `test_partial_signing_settings_fail_before_packaging` checks incomplete inputs. The original script failed nine fixture assertions. Shell parsing, release workflow YAML parsing, the five secret mappings, and the commit whitespace check passed.\n\nA second test used native macOS tools and three small universal Mach-O programs. It extracted the ZIP and mounted the DMG read-only. It verified ad hoc signatures, the arm64 and x86_64 slices of each program, the icon, an example, and the Applications link. This test exposed an incompatible two-architecture lipo check. The final script checks each architecture separately.\n\n## Limits\n\nThe Developer ID and notary service tests used simulated credentials and command responses. No production identity, Apple submission, release upload, or hosted workflow ran. A maintainer with the release credentials must confirm Apple's acceptance on a signed build.\n\nChangelog: macOS release packages can use Developer ID signing and Apple notarization when the release credentials are configured.\n\nEffort: medium\n\nFixes #1642\n",
